  </worksheet>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Interlude: Scientific Notation on a Calculator</title>
    <p>
      Some calculators and computers have their own way to display scientific notation. Try entering the numbers from the practice problems with the most zeros and see
      what happens. Your calculator likely converted it it scientific notation. Some calculators will display <m>\times 10^n</m> like we write it or you may have
      a small E in place of the <m>\times 10</m>. Spreadsheets usually use a lower case e for scientific notation. If you do a lot of number crunching, you will likely run 
      into scientific notation on your calculator at some point, so it is good to familiarize yourself on what your calculator does.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here a couple of videos on how to use scientific notation on different Texas Instrument calculators
      
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
</section> 